      "writtenOn": "2026-01-14T16:30:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, that does look incorrect. I took a careful look at SkSLMetalCodeGenerator and its `findLSB` emulation is correct (i.e. `ctz` plus a special case for 0 -\u003e -1). The `findMSB` emulation correctly handles flipping the sign of negative inputs (e.g. v \u003c 0 matches findMSB(~v)), but it needs to be subtracted from N-1.\n\nThe other issue I noticed was that SkSL types both `findLSB` and `findMSB` to return `$genIType` even if the inputs are `$genUType`. This matches GLSL convention and means the -1 return value is well-defined even if called on an unsigned input. This is not the return type that Metal uses for `clz` or `ctz`.  Our codegen does  a cast in `findMSB` but doesn\u0027t in `findLSB`, which would then generate MSL failures.\n\nI also realized that WGSL code will need to have these same casts because it documents `findTrailingBit` and the unsigned `findLeadingBit` to be `T(-1)` so its return type matches that of its input.",
      "parentUuid": "fcf6a8f5_f0f9232e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 22,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 32,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "9748c868189f2986ff68796f96135a174a568c16",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
